  <h2>Project Sunrise and the Geography of Isolation</h2>
  <p>Project Sunrise is Qantas's plan to connect Sydney nonstop with London and New York. The name recalls the airline's wartime “Double Sunrise” services across the Indian Ocean, whose occupants saw two sunrises during exceptionally long journeys. The modern version addresses the same geographic fact: Australia sits far from the largest European and North American markets.</p>
  <p>Today, a Sydney–London passenger may connect through Singapore, Dubai, Doha, or another Asian or Gulf hub. A Sydney–New York passenger may change aircraft in Los Angeles or elsewhere in North America. Project Sunrise removes that intermediate airport from selected itineraries.</p>
  <p>It does not eliminate hub-and-spoke aviation. Sydney remains the origin hub, and Qantas will still need Australian feeder traffic to fill 238 seats. London and New York are unusually deep markets with premium demand at both ends. Ultra-long-haul flights selectively bypass hubs on city pairs that can support them; they do not reproduce the connectivity that Singapore, Dubai, Doha, or Los Angeles offers to dozens of smaller destinations. A bypass can reduce transfer traffic and the centrality of an intermediate hub on one path while leaving the wider network intact.</p>
  <figure class="article-photo">
