  </figure>

  <h2>Four Hours Saved—But at What Cost?</h2>
  <p>Qantas has promoted a roughly four-hour saving compared with current one-stop journeys. That claim makes more sense at the itinerary level than in the air. A connection adds descent and taxi time, deplaning, terminal walking, security or transfer formalities, a scheduled buffer, baggage handling, another boarding process, and the risk that a late first flight breaks the second. Removing that sequence can reduce both clock time and psychological friction.</p>
  <p>For an executive whose time is expensive, four hours and one fewer failure point can justify a substantial fare premium. For a price-sensitive traveler, the stop may be a useful chance to walk, eat, or split the physical burden—and a one-stop fare may remain much cheaper. “Faster” describes the itinerary. It does not settle which experience offers better value.</p>

  <figure class="duration-chart" aria-labelledby="duration-title">
