  <h2>The Crew Fatigue Problem</h2>
  <p>Passenger discomfort is a product issue; crew alertness is a safety system. Ultra-long-range operations require augmented crews, protected rest opportunities, and a Fatigue Risk Management System (FRMS) that treats fatigue as a measurable operational hazard. ICAO describes FRMS as a data-driven process grounded in science and experience, and its ULR guidance recommends a fully augmented flight crew with planned in-flight rest.</p>
  <p>The Airbus test is informative but should not be mistaken for Qantas's final roster. Five pilots were aboard the Melbourne–Toulouse development leg, including Qantas observers, and they used staggered rotations. Because test instrumentation occupied the dedicated pilot-rest spaces, the pilots slept in the eight-berth cabin-crew rest compartment. A scheduled service will have passengers, cabin crew, commercial duties, and an operator-approved roster.</p>
  <p>The difficult moment is not hour ten at cruise. It is a complex arrival after rest that may have been short, fragmented, or mistimed against the body clock. ICAO notes continuing scientific uncertainty around very long duty periods. In ultra-long-haul aviation, fuel capacity may no longer be the primary constraint. The quality of human sleep may be.</p>

  <h2>Why the Aircraft Has Only 238 Seats</h2>
  <p>Airbus currently lists the standard A350-1000 at approximately 350–410 seats in a typical three-class configuration, although actual airline layouts vary. Qantas will install only 238 seats on its Project Sunrise aircraft: six First suites, 52 Business seats, 40 Premium Economy seats, and 140 Economy seats, plus the Wellbeing Zone. That is less a spacious version of a normal layout than a different revenue machine.</p>
